WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.752
Least privilege means a database account has only the access needed for
its approved purpose.

2
00:00:04.752 --> 00:00:14.797
A public web application may need to read and write specific tables, but
it usually should not create schemas, manage users, change permissions,
disable audit settings, or read unrelated data sets.

3
00:00:14.797 --> 00:00:17.203
A reporting process may need read-only access.

4
00:00:17.203 --> 00:00:21.414
A migration job may need schema-change privileges for a short operational
window.

5
00:00:21.414 --> 00:00:25.624
Administrative credentials should not live inside normal application
runtimes.

6
00:00:25.624 --> 00:00:27.669
Separate accounts make intent visible.

7
00:00:27.669 --> 00:00:35.790
Read-only, read-write, migration, reporting, maintenance, and
administrative identities should not be treated as cosmetic labels on the
same broad access.

8
00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:41.384
Their privileges should reflect what the workload is allowed to do, and
their use should be observable in logs.

9
00:00:41.384 --> 00:00:52.752
When an application account performs an unexpected schema change, connects
from an unexpected environment, or touches data outside its purpose, that
should stand out rather than blend into normal administrator activity.

10
00:00:52.752 --> 00:00:55.158
Environment separation is equally important.

11
00:00:55.158 --> 00:01:03.038
Development, staging, and production should use different credentials,
different databases or schemas as appropriate, and different access
policies.

12
00:01:03.038 --> 00:01:08.813
A local script should not accidentally point at production because a
copied environment file had a real endpoint.

13
00:01:08.813 --> 00:01:11.399
CI jobs should not inherit developer credentials.

14
00:01:11.399 --> 00:01:16.512
Staging data should not create privacy risk simply because it feels less
important than production.

15
00:01:16.512 --> 00:01:20.061
Separation reduces blast radius and makes mistakes easier to detect.

16
00:01:20.061 --> 00:01:25.414
Modern platforms may also support workload identity, short-lived tokens,
or managed service accounts.

17
00:01:25.414 --> 00:01:34.317
Those patterns can reduce the need for long-lived static passwords, but
they do not remove the need for database permissions, environment
boundaries, and rotation planning.

18
00:01:34.317 --> 00:01:43.640
The principle is stable: the connection identity should match the
application purpose, carry only necessary privileges, and be revocable
without guessing which systems will break.
